////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Acid Test: Chain Abbreviations
//
// In almost every position a transitive chain is shorthand for the
// conjunction of its trios, and processChainAbbreviations()
// (interpret.js) expands it into trios of the chain's own polarity
// during interpretation.  The one chain that is NOT an abbreviation
// is a chain claimed as a conclusion: that is a proof step the user
// expects to see treated row by row, with `by` reasons, substitution
// diffs and per-row feedback, and splitChains() still handles those
// at validation time.  isAnAbbreviatingChain() in extensions.js draws
// exactly that line.
//
// Covered here beyond plain assumptions (see 'Given Chains'):
//
//  - a chain as a Let's such-that body.  The `be such that` shorthand
//    turns a comma list into an environment body, so the chain is a
//    CLAIM inside a GIVEN environment - neither a conclusion nor a
//    given, and therefore invisible to both of the older passes.
//  - a chain as a Rule's conclusion, which becomes one conclusion per
//    step.
//  - the boundaries of each.
//
// Companion files: 'Given Chains', 'Given Chains Absent', 'Given
// Chains Params', 'Set Builder Chains'.
//
// Each section below uses its own disjoint constants so that rules
// and instantiations cannot cross-justify between sections.

Declare ≤, <, =, ∈, 0
Rule: :{ ChainsRule }

➤ "a typed Let whose such-that body is a chain"
{
  Declare b1
  { Let r1 ∈ ℤ, 0 ≤ r1 < b1
    r1 ∈ ℤ ✔︎
    0 ≤ r1 ✔︎
    r1 < b1 ✔︎
    0 < b1 ✔︎
  }
}

➤ "a bare Let whose such-that body is a chain, and the converse guard"
{
  Declare b2
  { Let r2, 0 ≤ r2 < b2
    0 ≤ r2 ✔︎
    r2 < b2 ✔︎
    b2 ≤ r2 ✗
  }
}

➤ "a chain in a Let-environment Rule premise, satisfied by the user's Let"
{
  Declare b3, P3, G3
  Rule: :{ :{ Let x ∈ ℤ, 0 ≤ x < b3  P3(x) }  G3 }
  Rule: :{ :y < b3  P3(y) }
  { Let r3 ∈ ℤ, 0 ≤ r3 < b3
    r3 < b3 ✔︎
    P3(r3) ✔︎
  }
  G3 ✔︎
}

// A Let's propositional identity comes from its ORIGINAL body, which
// this pass deliberately leaves untouched (that putdown is the
// declaration's ProperName signature).  So the two spellings of a
// such-that body are NOT interchangeable: a rule premise written with
// commas is not satisfied by a user's Let written as a chain, even
// though their body copies expand to the same trios.  Recorded here
// as the current boundary; note the premise below is not vacuous, so
// this really is the Let atoms failing to match.

➤ "but the two spellings of a such-that body do not share an atom"
{
  Declare b4, P4, G4
  Rule: :{ :{ Let x ∈ ℤ, 0 ≤ x, x < b4  P4(x) }  G4 }
  Rule: :{ :y < b4  P4(y) }
  { Let r4 ∈ ℤ, 0 ≤ r4 < b4
    r4 < b4 ✔︎
    P4(r4) ✔︎
  }
  G4 ✗
}

➤ "a Rule concluding a chain concludes each of its steps"
{
  Declare A5, u5, v5, w5
  Rule: :{ :A5(x,y,z)  x = y < z }
  Assume A5(u5,v5,w5)
  u5 = v5 ✔︎
  v5 < w5 ✔︎
  w5 < u5 ✗
}

// A Rule's chain contains metavariables, so no ground transitivity
// instantiation can be built from it, and the chain is removed from
// the Rule rather than left inert.  The transitive conclusion of a
// rule-concluded chain is therefore not supplied by the rule itself;
// the user gets it by writing the chain in their own proof, where it
// is an ordinary conclusion chain.  Only one claim follows the Assume
// below, because an accessible earlier claim is treated as a given
// and two claimed steps plus transitivity would make the third valid.

➤ "a Rule's own chain does not supply its transitive conclusion"
{
  Declare A6, u6, v6, w6
  Rule: :{ :A6(x,y,z)  x = y < z }
  Assume A6(u6,v6,w6)
  u6 < w6 ✗
}

➤ "the user recovers it by claiming the chain, an ordinary proof step"
{
  Declare A7, u7, v7, w7
  Rule: :{ :A7(x,y,z)  x = y < z }
  Assume A7(u7,v7,w7)
  { u7 = v7 < w7 ✔︎
    u7 < w7 ✔︎
  }
}
